Trail (@trail: 0, is #chapter, #instinct, #part-of @ontology, #done) { r_Q46Z5FZHZFACPNSNPJP2GEDC1D
Alexandria was a library inside a temple, well maintained and kept, in a city with a port, many travelers where asked to hand their books and manuscripts and receive a copy instead. While there is the common imagination that it was burned, the details are a little conflicting. What remains from the story is the idea of a great body of knowledge, that worked because it was cared for, and the fact that it can suddenly catch fire and be lost. Great care can be put into maintaining and expanding knowledge. It will most likely provide itself useful if used for the meeting of our Needs.

In Lince, the Alexandria vibe means sharing Records as knowledge of what things are, how they work, their consequences, and how to implement them.

That in turn means possibly caring for the building of interfaces and components to access knowledge, learn it and help use it while also helping with the management of knowledge: writing it and sharing it.

We've come a long way, we should record that, the Trail we made should be known by others. We hope people can learn from us. Catch up on latest wisdom and knowledge. Understand how something works, how to build it, reproduce it, make it better, turn it into their own. Trail is how we call the exporting and importing of Lince data.

It should come in as a package, one can put into the db and start using it in a useful way right away. It should be able to come with Records that describe what things are, how they work, what they need to do to make it work. How can they prove it is real/right. What tasks, recurrent or not, need to be made for it to function, what processes of transfer could/should be made for it to be actualized. The steps for something to be achieved, refined over centuries, by billions of people, with normal Lince workflows of collective improvement. People create useful patterns, import information from other systems, all we need to turn every piece of wisdom and knowledge from the world into something we can hold is a button for exporting part of the data you hold, and a button to import what you want.

This isnt a big feature, it is an abstraction, a dream. A small thing to turn data into value, a Trail one can follow, with steps ranging from online personal education to how to build a school, how to organize the learning of the teachers and how do they rotate the student's learning across several subjects for maximum development. A good Lince will allow for this Trail to be edited, agreed upon, by many people, before it is is unwrapped and it's tasks assigned, and the work done by each envolved.

In this document you may see checkboxes, for each of the Trails that want to be built. The first one is Instinct, the tutorial of Lince that comes with every copy, as a Sand with text that can turn into Records, for one to manage the progress of their knowledge of Lince, some may be in -1 quantity, needing to be read and understood, others are 1 quantity, done... There might be various ways to import data into Lince, they may all be called a Trail one can follow, or diverge from. There is a lot of work to build a body of knowledge capable of supporting a society from scratch, that's a prerequisite for Lince to sustain itself, right...? Let's pretend that we do need a highly organized, advanced and functional society for Lince to be actualized, then it is Lince's job to make sure that those that want to build it have the means necessary for doing so, and the knowledge for that, from the chopping of wood, to the iron and up to the end must be well documented. That means an open university style of knowledge accumulation, with ways to embed that into a Lince when building it's code or linking it to be accessed when using Lince. Since we are going to be pretentious we need to deliver, we must make the tool a silver bullet for reaching the philosophy, complete.

Now the question remains, how do we integrate with other systems, how do we make sure we can download wikipedia and other bases of knowledge and Lince's own file system for data storage and put all into one place that gets ingested as one or at least interacted in Lince as if it where all the same thing, turning the many different sources of information into actionable tasks and knowledge?

Trail can be a sand and a system thought. Today if i want to have a certain Trail (bundle of data i want) i need usually either seed it into the db or put in a Sand and load it into the db. Could it be more efficient? Could we the same way we ingest .lingua files for Instinct just dump .lingua files as plaintext in the lince-config-dir/trail/ or something? In the end they will probably be .lingua files, importing them is passing them around and evaluating them? Or having them in the disk and deciding to use them? Like a cardgame, i evoke the 'Computer Science.lingua' Trail, now i have a billion things to study, and/or I point to a public sand that is updated with a lot of knowledge of a kind i want and i pull whatever is tagged as a @trail and @computer-science and other @concepts to make sure it is what i want.

Trails of certain types cannot be maintained by non-technical people of such area probably, one can teach itself, but it should be curated, and updated, as these fields have advancements everyday. A good Lince will be able to remove friction between generating and prooving things inside Lince or not, and having them accessible as a Trail, managing data is a Need, we should attend to it.

Here are the subjects wanted to create Trails for:
- [ ] Basic Economic Transfers - these might really like certain features that have some specific combinations, being useful to make a Castle that is an easy way to enact certain workflows. People can download these Trails for living in cities, as templates on how to use Lince for doing common things like buying seats at an event, cinema, restaurant reservations, or ride sharing, supermarket purchases, all as preset workflows, Trails.
