## Architecture references

**Turso** (`testing/simulator/` in their repo) is the closest existing
implementation, in four layers:
1. Simulator (`main.rs`) — entry point; generates random configs and
   interaction plans, runs them sequentially or concurrently.
2. Model (`model.rs`) — a simplified, memory-resident "what the DB should
   contain"; tracks atomic actions as the source of truth to check against.
3. Generation (`generation.rs`) — pseudo-randomly generates interaction
   plans, mock tables, schema workloads from a configured distribution.
4. Properties (`properties.rs`) — invariants (transaction atomicity,
   linearizability, isolation levels), checked at every simulation step.

Mechanics: all I/O (network, file writes, time delays) routes through the simulation layer instead of hitting the OS directly, so nothing can bypass the seed. The seed is what lets a bug found after millions of randomized operations be replayed exactly. Fault injection: dropped packets, paused threads, delayed commits, nodes killed mid-write, to stress the concurrent engine. Turso additionally pairs its in-house DST with [Antithesis](https://antithesis.com/), a deterministic hypervisor that injects low-level OS/hardware faults — a second, independent layer for catching what a custom simulator's own blind spots miss, not a substitute for it.

**TigerBeetle** — reference for the discipline behind "everything a node waits on has to be something the harness can drive" (TigerStyle).

**patchbay crate** — flagged as possibly useful for simulating the network layer specifically; not yet investigated.

**octopii** (also named in `Resenha.lingua`) — candidate for the time / network / disk simulation plumbing itself: https://github.com/octopii-rs/octopii

